// =============================================================================
// MASES UI — Root TypeScript configuration
// =============================================================================
//
// File:    mase_app/tsconfig.json
// Phase:   1 (Frontend foundation) — Unit 1.2
// Version: 1.0.1
// Status:  PROVISIONAL — the pinned TypeScript major is confirmed by
//          Phase 0 Unit 0.1 Part E. The structure below is final.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Revision history
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.0.0  — initial delivery.
// 1.0.1  — the `references` block was removed. TypeScript 5.7 (the
//          pinned major) rejects a composite project with `noEmit:
//          true` when that project is the target of a reference:
//          error TS6310. The reference was IDE-tooling-only and was
//          never followed by `vue-tsc --noEmit` — see the header
//          comment below. Removing it has no effect on the build or
//          on the files type-checked. See `tsconfig.node.json`'s
//          header comment, §"Why noEmit + composite together", for
//          the anticipated fix that this revision applies.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What this file governs
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// This is the APPLICATION project. It covers:
//
//     src/**               — every .ts, .d.ts, and .vue file
//     workspace-entry/**   — the workspace entry point .ts
//     portal-entry/**      — the portal entry point .ts
//     vite-env.d.ts        — the ambient types file (Unit 1.4)
//
// It does NOT cover:
//
//     vite.config.ts       — covered by tsconfig.node.json (Unit 1.3)
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Project references — REMOVED in v1.0.1
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The v1.0.0 file carried a `references` block:
//
//     "references": [{ "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }]
//
// Its only purpose was to associate vite.config.ts with
// tsconfig.node.json for the VS Code language server. That association
// is not required for the build (Vite transforms the config file
// itself) and was never followed by `vue-tsc --noEmit` at the root.
//
// TypeScript 5.7 rejects the referenced project's combination of
// `composite: true` and `noEmit: true` with TS6310. Two fixes were
// possible: let the referenced project emit declaration files into a
// temp directory, or stop referencing it. The v1.0.1 revision applies
// the second fix — the file's own documented "option (a)" — because it
// is the simpler of the two and preserves every build-time behavior.
//
// IDE tooling still works: VS Code selects `tsconfig.node.json` for
// vite.config.ts by the file's own `include: ["vite.config.ts"]`
// pattern, which does not depend on the project-reference mechanism.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Compiler target and library
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `target: ES2022` matches modern evergreen browsers and the Node LTS
// line used for development. The Vite build itself targets ES2020
// (vite.config.ts Unit 1.5) — the difference is intentional: TypeScript
// is free to use newer syntax in its own analysis, and Vite lowers the
// emitted syntax to the broader ES2020 baseline.
//
// `lib` covers both the language primitives (ES2022) and the browser
// environment (DOM, DOM.Iterable).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module system
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The project is ESM. `package.json` declares `"type": "module"`; the
// emitted syntax and the module resolution behavior both follow.
//
// `moduleResolution: "bundler"` is the correct mode for a project built
// by Vite. It allows extension-less imports of ESM packages, honors
// package.json `exports` maps, and works with the `.vue` import path
// that vue-tsc understands.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Strictness
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `strict: true` enables the baseline family:
//
//     noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks, strictFunctionTypes,
//     strictBindCallApply, strictPropertyInitialization,
//     noImplicitThis, alwaysStrict, useUnknownInCatchVariables
//
// Added explicitly on top of strict:
//
//     noImplicitOverride     — subclass overrides must use `override`
//     noUnusedLocals         — dead imports and locals fail the build
//     noUnusedParameters     — dead parameters fail the build
//
// One known interaction: AppMobileDrawer.vue (Unit 11.17) imports
// useAuthStore with an ESLint disable comment for "unused for side
// effect". TypeScript's noUnusedLocals does not honor that comment and
// will flag the import at Phase 3 verification. A v1.0.1 re-issue of
// that file removes the import; see the phase plan §5 (bounded re-issue
// policy). Recorded here so the failure is anticipated.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Isolated modules and verbatim module syntax
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `isolatedModules` is required by Vite: each file is transpiled in
// isolation, so TypeScript must not rely on cross-file type inference
// to resolve imports.
//
// `verbatimModuleSyntax` enforces the `import type` / `export type`
// discipline the delivered source already follows. It prevents a
// future contribution from accidentally importing a type as a value
// (which would confuse the bundler's dead-code elimination) or a value
// as a type (which would confuse the type-checker).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// JSX and class fields
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `jsx: "preserve"` is standard for the Vite + Vue + TS stack. It
// defers JSX transformation to the Vue plugin.
//
// `useDefineForClassFields: true` aligns class-field semantics with
// ESNext. No delivered source uses class fields, so the flag is inert
// today but correct for future contributions.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Interop and resolution
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `esModuleInterop: true` and `allowSyntheticDefaultImports: true`
// allow default imports from CommonJS packages (axios is the relevant
// case; it ships ESM wrappers but the type definitions historically
// assumed CommonJS interop).
//
// `resolveJsonModule: true` allows `import data from './x.json'`. No
// delivered file uses it, but it is standard for Vite projects and
// costs nothing.
//
// `forceConsistentCasingInFileNames: true` prevents cross-platform
// case-mismatch bugs (macOS and Windows filesystems are case-
// insensitive; Linux is case-sensitive).
//
// `skipLibCheck: true` skips type-checking of `.d.ts` files in
// node_modules. This is the correct default for an application (as
// opposed to a library author): the third-party `.d.ts` files are
// assumed correct, and checking them slows the build without catching
// errors in your own code.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Path alias
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `@/*` → `./src/*`. This must match vite.config.ts (Unit 1.5) exactly.
// The build's `resolve.alias` and TypeScript's `paths` are two halves
// of the same rule; a divergence between them would make one tool pass
// and the other fail.
//
// `baseUrl: "."` is required for `paths` to resolve relative to the
// repository root.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Ambient types
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `types: ["vite/client"]` restricts the ambient type packages
// TypeScript auto-includes to `vite/client` only. This provides:
//
//     import.meta.env.DEV       (used in BaseIcon.vue, Unit 8.19)
//     import.meta.env.PROD
//     import.meta.env.MODE
//     import.meta.glob / .globEager
//     the .vue module declaration (*.vue imports)
//
// It deliberately does NOT include `@types/node`. If a delivered
// src/ file needed a Node global (process, Buffer, __dirname, fs),
// that would indicate an architectural error — src/ is browser code.
// The absence of @types/node in this project makes that class of
// mistake impossible to introduce without editing this file.
//
// =============================================================================

{
  "compilerOptions": {
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Target and library
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": [
      "ES2022",
      "DOM",
      "DOM.Iterable"
    ],

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Module system
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Strictness
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "strict": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // JSX and class-field semantics
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "jsx": "preserve",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Interop and resolution
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "allowSyntheticDefaultImports": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Emit — the build is handled by Vite; TypeScript does not emit
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "noEmit": true,

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Path alias — must match vite.config.ts's `resolve.alias` exactly
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    },

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Ambient type packages
    // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    "types": ["vite/client"]
  },

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Project contents
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Every source tree that belongs to the frontend application. The
  // entry HTML files (workspace-entry/index.html, portal-entry/index.html)
  // are NOT included — they are not TypeScript. Vite references them
  // via vite.config.ts's rollupOptions.input.
  "include": [
    "src/**/*.ts",
    "src/**/*.d.ts",
    "src/**/*.vue",
    "workspace-entry/**/*.ts",
    "portal-entry/**/*.ts",
    "vite-env.d.ts"
  ],

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Exclusions
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // node_modules and the two build output directories. The `exclude`
  // here is conventional; TypeScript's default behavior already
  // ignores node_modules, and the build directories contain no
  // TypeScript source.
  "exclude": [
    "node_modules",
    "dist",
    "dist-portal"
  ]

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Project references — REMOVED in v1.0.1
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The v1.0.0 file ended with:
  //
  //     "references": [
  //       { "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" }
  //     ]
  //
  // That block is removed. TypeScript 5.7 rejects a referenced project
  // that combines `composite: true` with `noEmit: true` — error
  // TS6310 — and the reference served only IDE tooling, which no
  // longer needs it. See the header comment §"Project references —
  // REMOVED in v1.0.1".
}